Z||000012||GTMike||08-31-2001||04:12 PM||||i agree.  the tdc 3.4 is alot of work.   thats why i'm going with the push rod.  and the 2.8 heads and intake arnt junk they are the same heads that are on the 3.4 pushrod. you don't have to splice wiring harnesses and use other computers.   its only 20 hp less. you can make that up with a cam,t-body,porting,possibalby a pully.  i already have a ram air intake with no restrictions that takes cool air in from outside the engine compartment.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||c m west||08-31-2001||04:24 PM||||for those interested, I can supply BRAND NEW, in the crate GM 3.4 Long Blocks for $1700<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||87GTZ34||08-31-2001||04:26 PM||||Actually, the 3.4 TDC/DOHC is probably more work than the caddy.  You have to modify the trunk hinge, the remove the bracket for the dogbone and modify a set of holes for the rear trans mount.  On top of that is changing over all of the electronics and some of the plumbing for the TDC.  The best upgrade for the work/buck is the 3.4 push rod engine.||||reg||1
Z||000015||dennis_6||08-31-2001||04:30 PM||||I am open to other ideals, but the caddy swap looked really hard.  You have to get parts from atleast 3 different cars and fabricate alot of parts yourself.  And getting the caddy PCM to work with the fiero dash does not sound like fun to me.  As for the 3.4 TDC I did not want to put the fiero heads or intake on the 3.4TDC.  What would be so hard about using the 3.4's computer spliced with the fiero's computer and keeping the 3.4's intake and heads.  Everyone keeps saying that is to much trouble but I don't see any problems other than having to cut the decklid support.||||reg||4
